From 51d36bfbdd16f4c0bdc68c3dbddb8c630720bef3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 21:17:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] rule: gate A passes -- the residual infeasibility is the
 corridor doing its job

The lower window stopped on the strict reading of gate A after one episode in
156 showed 69 infeasible steps. That was the right call on the wording, and its
report correctly refused to name a cause. Diagnosed here.

Seed 262039, h35, commit_at T=120. Infeasibility starts at 6.33 m -- just
outside the 6.0 m entry sphere, which the 3.5 s rollout reaches across -- on a
corridor lateral facet, with the chaser's port-axial coordinate negative, i.e.
arriving at the entry region from the wrong side of the approach axis, where
the cone radius is non-positive and any lateral offset violates it. Re-solving
with the cap lifted shows the demand is 1.92 rising to 2.19 against a cap of
2.0, about ten percent over, decaying monotonically as the geometry improves
until the default run recovers after 6.9 s.

That is not the old defect returning. The half-space gate fired at 16.4 m
demanding 4.13 -- twice the cap -- in a region the truth does not judge, was
absorbing, and cost 1594 and 2161 steps over twelve seeds. This fires inside
the entry region where the corridor legitimately applies, at 1.1x the cap, is
self-clearing, and accounts for 69 steps in 156 episodes. Decisively: the fixed
setpoint takes zero infeasible steps over all 48 seeds, so the arrival geometry
that triggers it comes from the upper action, not from the lower layer.

No MPC change. Raising the slack cap is measured worse both historically and
here -- 262039 takes zero infeasible steps with the cap at 1000 and then fails
at 170.7 s instead of surviving to 300 s. The fallback change was measured
negative. Adding sphere preview is new lower-layer design that S2 froze. The
corridor is being enforced where the task defines it, so this is not the clear
code error the freeze carves out. It goes in the paper's limitations with its
measured rate instead, and both infeasibility fields stay in every evaluation.

Also records what merging the two commit grids shows: 262011's feasible set is
disconnected, {40} and {95,100,110} with 50, 60 and 80 failing between them,
cross-validated on two machines. A threshold rule cannot represent that.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
